  "evidence": "TEXT_E: Triple NHS investment with 18‑week treatment target; halve child poverty via tax credits, childcare and Sure Start; extend paid maternity leave and flexible working; smoking restrictions in enclosed workplaces and curbs on junk food advertising; neighbourhood policing, SOCA, control orders and ID cards; retain independent nuclear deterrent and prioritise veterans. TEXT_F: Increase NHS funding by £34bn and emphasise matrons/infection control; stronger school discipline and ‘honesty in sentencing’ with more police/prison places; strict border controls and migration limits; tax cuts and deregulation; partial NHS rebates for private operations; defence uplift and EU referendum.",
  "reasoning": "Because Care/Harm is the electorate’s strongest foundation, TEXT_E’s heavy emphasis on NHS expansion, child poverty reduction, family support and public‑health measures aligns best, while still addressing Authority/Loyalty through robust policing, security powers and defence commitments. TEXT_F places relatively more weight on authority, sovereignty and market mechanisms, offering less comprehensive care‑oriented redistribution and public‑health action than TEXT_E.",
